@@ PE1 | Agile
The teams of an online retailer optimise different metrics, such as page views, app downloads and clicks on promotions, and their priorities often pull in different directions. What would best align them?
A) A single outcome metric linked to customer value, supported by a few team-level measures
B) A longer list of metrics, so that every team can show progress on the measures it prefers
C) A ranking of the teams by velocity, so that the fastest teams set the overall direction
D) A rule that teams must get the sponsor's approval before changing any of their metrics
= A
? A shared "north star" outcome metric gives all teams a common direction, while supporting metrics show how each team contributes. More metrics or output-based rankings do not create alignment.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
Leadership wants each team's quarterly work to support company goals while leaving teams free to decide how to reach them. Which approach supports this best?
A) Objectives and key results set collaboratively at each level
B) Detailed annual task lists assigned to each team by the executives
C) Individual bonuses tied to the number of tasks each person completes
D) A single company-wide project plan that every team must follow exactly
= A
? OKRs connect team objectives to organisational goals and measure outcomes, while teams keep the freedom to decide how to achieve them.

@@ PE1 | Agile
A new agile team will start a nine-month initiative next week. Which activity best builds a shared understanding of the initiative's purpose, context and ways of working?
A) A chartering (lift-off) workshop with the team and key stakeholders
B) An email from the sponsor that explains the business case in detail
C) A detailed work breakdown structure prepared by the project manager
D) A training course on the Scrum framework for all of the team members
= A
? A lift-off or chartering workshop lets the team and stakeholders explore purpose, alignment and context together, which creates shared understanding and commitment.

@@ PE1 | Agile
Stakeholders and developers describe the customer journey for a hotel booking app very differently, and their discussions keep going in circles. Which technique would best create a shared view?
A) User story mapping of the customer journey together with the stakeholders
B) A detailed requirements document written by the business analyst alone
C) A list of features ranked by the most senior stakeholder in the room
D) A vote on which version of the customer journey the team prefers
= A
? A story map visualises the user journey and the work that supports each step, so stakeholders and the team build the same mental picture and can slice releases together.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
On a water utility's smart-meter programme, the field crews install meters to a fixed plan, while an agile team builds the data platform. Each group dismisses the other's way of working. What should the project manager do?
A) Bring both groups together around the shared customer outcome and show how their work connects
B) Keep the two groups apart, since their methods are too different to work together effectively
C) Ask the field crews to adopt Scrum so that the whole programme uses a single way of working
D) Let the sponsor decide which group's way of working is correct, so that the dispute is settled
= A
? A common vision connects groups that use different approaches. Joint planning, shared demos and a clear common outcome help each group see the value of the other's work.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
A research project brings together university scientists, who want to publish, and an industry partner, who wants to patent the results. What should the project manager facilitate early in the project?
A) Agreement on shared objectives and on rules for publication and intellectual property
B) A decision by the sponsor about which partner's interests will take priority
C) A postponement of the discussion until the first results are available
D) Separate plans for each partner, so that their interests do not have to be reconciled
= A
? Building a common vision includes agreeing how different interests will be handled. Agreeing objectives and publication and IP rules early prevents later disputes.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
Before planning a new hotel's opening, the project manager learns that the department heads have different ideas of what "ready to open" means. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate agreement on measurable opening criteria and document them
B) Use the criteria that the general manager prefers, since she runs the hotel
C) Let each department decide for itself when its own area is ready to open
D) Define the criteria after construction, when the building's state is clearer
= A
? Clear, agreed acceptance criteria for the end state give everyone the same target and are documented in the scope statement.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Two researchers argue about whose name should appear first on the project's main publication. The project has an agreed authorship policy. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a discussion that applies the agreed policy
B) Decide the order personally to end the dispute quickly
C) Ask the journal's editor to decide which researcher should be listed first
D) Remove both names from the paper and publish it under the project's name
= A
? Agreed policies provide an objective basis for resolving disputes. Facilitating a discussion based on them keeps the decision fair and transparent.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
A hotel stays open during its renovation. The general manager demands that noisy work stop before 10 a.m., while the contractor says this will delay the project by three weeks. What should the project manager do?
A) Explore options with both, such as resequencing noisy work or using quieter methods
B) Side with the general manager, since guest satisfaction matters more than the schedule
C) Side with the contractor, since the schedule baseline has already been approved
D) Ask the hotel owner to decide, since the general manager and the contractor disagree
= A
? Collaborating to find solutions that meet both needs is the preferred approach when both concerns are important and there is time to explore options.

@@ PE2 | Agile
Team members have split into two factions, and each side now cares more about winning the argument than about solving the problem. Which level of conflict does this describe?
A) Contest
B) Problem to solve
C) Disagreement
D) World war
= A
? In the levels-of-conflict model, a contest is reached when winning becomes more important than solving the problem and factions form. Earlier levels focus on the problem or on self-protection.

@@ PE2 | Agile
The marketing lead wants pop-up promotions on the online shop's checkout page, while the UX designer says they will reduce sales. The discussion has become heated. What should the product owner do?
A) Propose an experiment, such as an A/B test, and decide based on the results
B) Accept the marketing lead's request, since promotions are part of marketing's role
C) Accept the UX designer's view, since design decisions belong to the design specialists
D) Postpone the decision until the two colleagues are able to agree with each other
= A
? When a disagreement can be tested, data from an experiment provides an objective basis for the decision and moves the discussion away from opinions.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
The installers' union objects to the weekend shifts that the project planned in order to meet the meter rollout schedule. What should the project manager do?
A) Meet the union early to understand its concerns and negotiate options within the labour agreement
B) Keep the weekend shifts, since the schedule baseline was approved by the steering committee
C) Hire contractors for the weekends without discussing it with the union
D) Remove the weekend shifts and accept whatever delay results from the change
= A
? Engaging the union early and negotiating within the labour agreement addresses the concern and looks for options that protect both the workers' interests and the schedule.

@@ PE2 | Agile
Developers in one office feel that they handle far more out-of-hours support calls than their colleagues in the other office, and tension is growing. What should the scrum master do?
A) Make the support workload visible and help the team agree a fair rotation
B) Tell the developers that the support load will even out over time
C) Ask the manager of the other office to assign more support calls to his people
D) Remove out-of-hours support from the team and give it to a separate group
= A
? Making the facts visible and letting the team agree a fair solution addresses the cause of the conflict and supports self-management.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Before a deadline, a project manager emphasises the points on which two team members agree and plays down their differences, to keep the peace. Which conflict approach is this?
A) Smoothing (accommodating)
B) Forcing (directing)
C) Compromising (reconciling)
D) Collaborating (problem solving)
= A
? Smoothing emphasises areas of agreement and plays down differences. It can preserve relationships in the short term, but the underlying issue may remain.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid | multi
Which two behaviours help a project manager handle conflict effectively? (Choose two.)
A) Addressing conflicts early, before they escalate
B) Focusing on the issues rather than on the personalities
C) Letting every conflict run its course without intervening
D) Referring every disagreement to human resources at once
E) Deciding all disputes personally to save the team's time
= A, B
? Early, issue-focused conflict management prevents escalation and keeps relationships intact. Ignoring conflict, escalating everything or imposing all decisions undermines the team.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
A new team member is very motivated but lacks the skills for the task she has been given. According to situational leadership, which style fits best?
A) Directing
B) Coaching
C) Supporting
D) Delegating
= A
? An enthusiastic beginner (high commitment, low competence) needs clear direction and close guidance. Coaching suits someone whose competence and commitment are both still developing.

@@ PE3 | Agile
In retrospectives, team members never mention their own mistakes. The scrum master wants to build psychological safety. What should the scrum master do first?
A) Model openness by sharing their own mistakes and thanking others for being candid
B) Ask each team member to write down one mistake anonymously before every retrospective
C) Report the lack of openness to the team's line manager so that it can be addressed
D) Tell the team that mistakes will no longer be discussed, to make everyone feel safe
= A
? Leaders build psychological safety by modelling vulnerability and responding positively to candour. Escalating or banning the topic makes people even more cautious.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
During a tense meeting, the project manager notices that she is becoming angry about a vendor's excuses for repeated delays. Which emotional intelligence skill does she most need at this moment?
A) Self-regulation
B) Social awareness
C) Motivation
D) Empathy
= A
? Self-regulation is the ability to manage one's own emotions and reactions, for example by pausing before responding. It follows self-awareness of the emotion.

@@ PE3 | Agile
The team wants a quick way to check how much support a proposal has before moving on. Which technique fits best?
A) Fist of five
B) The Delphi technique
C) A Pareto analysis
D) A Monte Carlo simulation
= A
? In fist of five, each person shows zero to five fingers to signal their level of support, which quickly reveals concerns that need discussion.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
A functional manager believes that team members avoid work unless they are closely supervised. Which theory describes this belief?
A) McGregor's Theory X
B) McGregor's Theory Y
C) Herzberg's two-factor theory
D) Maslow's hierarchy of needs
= A
? Theory X assumes that people dislike work and need close control. Theory Y assumes that people are self-motivated and seek responsibility, which fits empowered teams better.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
A team member who is hard of hearing struggles to follow video meetings, which have no captions. What should the project manager do?
A) Enable captions and agree inclusive meeting practices with the team
B) Ask the team member to read the meeting notes afterwards instead of attending
C) Wait until the team member raises a formal request through human resources
D) Hold all meetings by email so that no one has to rely on hearing
= A
? Leaders create an inclusive environment by removing barriers proactively and agreeing practices, such as captions and shared notes, that let everyone take part fully.

@@ PE3 | Agile
The scrum master wants to give a developer feedback about interrupting colleagues during meetings. Which approach is most effective?
A) Describe the situation, the behaviour and its impact, privately and soon after it happens
B) Mention the problem to the whole team at the next retrospective without naming anyone
C) Wait for the developer's annual review, so that all of the feedback can be given together
D) Ask a colleague who was interrupted to give the feedback instead of the scrum master
= A
? Feedback that is specific (situation, behaviour, impact), private and timely is easier to accept and act on.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
Field crews installing smart meters feel disconnected from the project office and say their feedback is ignored. What should the project manager do?
A) Visit the crews regularly, hold short field huddles and act visibly on their feedback
B) Send the crews a monthly newsletter that summarises the decisions of the project office
C) Ask the crew supervisors to collect complaints and send them in a quarterly report
D) Explain to the crews that their role is to install the meters, not to give feedback
= A
? Being present, listening and showing that feedback leads to action builds trust and engagement, especially with teams that work away from the project office.

@@ PE3 | Predictive | multi
Which two actions help a project manager build trust with a new team? (Choose two.)
A) Following through on commitments consistently
B) Being transparent about decisions and constraints
C) Keeping difficult news private to avoid worrying the team
D) Making all decisions personally to show strong leadership
E) Reviewing each team member's work in detail every day
= A, B
? Trust grows from reliability and transparency. Withholding information, centralising all decisions and micromanaging all erode trust.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A hotel renovation will affect long-stay guests and the shops in the hotel's arcade, but neither group is in the stakeholder register. What should the project manager do?
A) Add them to the register, analyse their interests and plan how to engage them
B) Leave them out, since they have no formal authority over the project's decisions
C) Ask the hotel's front desk to deal with any complaints from these groups as they arise
D) Inform them only at the end of the renovation, when the work has been completed
= A
? Stakeholder identification is ongoing. Groups that are affected by the project are added to the register, analysed and engaged according to their interests and influence.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
Some customers, such as elderly people, may struggle to use the new smart-meter app. How should the project engage them?
A) Involve representatives of these customers early in design and testing
B) Provide a printed manual after launch to anyone who asks for help
C) Assume that family members will help these customers use the app
D) Focus on the majority of customers, since a few groups cannot be served
= A
? Involving representatives of vulnerable groups early lets the design meet their needs and avoids exclusion and later complaints.

@@ PE4 | Predictive | match
Match each stakeholder engagement level with its description.
1) Unaware
2) Resistant
3) Neutral
4) Leading
A) Not aware of the project or its potential impacts
B) Aware of the project but opposed to the changes it brings
C) Aware of the project but neither supportive nor resistant
D) Actively engaged in helping the project to succeed
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? These levels are used in the stakeholder engagement assessment matrix to compare current and desired engagement. The fifth level, supportive, describes stakeholders who are aware and support the change.

@@ PE4 | Agile
The research funder wants to see results only at the end of a two-year project, but the team works in short experiments and needs regular guidance. What should the project manager do?
A) Invite the funder to periodic reviews of experiment results at a frequency the funder accepts
B) Send the funder every experiment's raw data each week, whether or not the funder asks for it
C) Wait until the end of the project, since the funder has said that this is what it prefers
D) Ask the team to make all decisions alone, since the funder does not want to be involved
= A
? Engagement is tailored to the stakeholder. Agreeing a review cadence that the funder accepts gives the team the guidance it needs without overloading the funder.

@@ PE4 | Agile
The team building an online shop has little direct contact with its customers. Which technique helps keep customers' needs in view during development?
A) Personas based on research and validated with real customers
B) Assumptions made by the developers about what customers want
C) A list of features requested by the sales director at the kickoff
D) A survey of the team members about their own shopping habits
= A
? Research-based personas represent real customer groups and help the team make decisions with users in mind. They should be validated and updated with real customer input.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
The project manager learns that an informal leader among the hotel's housekeeping staff strongly influences how her colleagues feel about the new room-management system. What should the project manager do?
A) Engage her early, for example as a change champion
B) Ignore her, since she has no formal authority
C) Ask her manager to tell her to support the project
D) Wait to see whether she supports or opposes the system
= A
? Informal leaders can have strong influence. Engaging them early, for example as champions, can turn their influence into support for the change.

@@ PE4 | Agile
Few stakeholders attend sprint reviews, saying that the sessions are long and technical. What should the scrum master and product owner do?
A) Keep reviews short and outcome-focused, and let stakeholders try the product themselves
B) Make attendance at sprint reviews compulsory for all stakeholders in the organisation
C) Replace the sprint reviews with a detailed written report sent at the end of each sprint
D) Hold reviews only at the end of each release, when there is more to show stakeholders
= A
? Reviews should engage stakeholders. Short, outcome-focused sessions with hands-on use of the product invite genuine feedback.

@@ PE4 | Predictive | multi
Which two sources are most useful for identifying stakeholders at the start of a project? (Choose two.)
A) The project charter
B) The business case
C) The final project report
D) The team's velocity history
E) The quality control measurements
= A, B
? The charter and business documents name the parties involved in and affected by the project. The other items are produced later or do not concern stakeholders.

@@ PE5 | Agile
At the start of a new e-commerce project, the project manager wants stakeholders to state explicitly which of scope, schedule, cost and quality is most flexible. Which technique fits best?
A) Trade-off sliders
B) Planning poker
C) A burndown chart
D) A risk register
= A
? Trade-off sliders make stakeholders rank which constraints can flex and which are fixed, which aligns expectations before decisions have to be made.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
The funders of a two-year research project expect a guaranteed breakthrough by the end of the project. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the uncertainty and agree milestones based on learning outcomes and decision points
B) Promise the breakthrough, since the funding could otherwise be withdrawn by the funders
C) Avoid discussing the expected results, since research outcomes cannot be predicted
D) Add a large time buffer to the schedule so that the breakthrough becomes more likely
= A
? Expectations should match the nature of the work. Research is uncertain, so success is defined through learning milestones and go/no-go decision points rather than guaranteed results.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
The hotel owner expects the renovated rooms to be bookable the day after construction ends, but the plan includes snagging, cleaning and inspections that take ten days. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the handover steps and agree a realistic availability date with the owner
B) Remove the inspections from the plan so that the owner's expectation can be met
C) Let the owner take bookings and deal with any problems when guests arrive
D) Say nothing now and explain the extra days once construction has finished
= A
? Expectations are aligned early by explaining the steps between completion and use. Skipping inspections or staying silent creates risk and later disappointment.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
Executives have told the board that smart meters will cut water losses by 25% in the first year. The engineers believe that 10% is realistic in the first year. What should the project manager do?
A) Share the evidence with the executives and agree a realistic benefits profile
B) Ask the engineers to revise their estimate so that it matches the executives' figure
C) Keep quiet, since the board will see the actual results at the end of the year
D) Report 25% in the plan and 10% in the team's internal documents
= A
? Benefit expectations must be realistic and evidence-based. The project manager raises the gap early so that the executives can correct expectations before more commitments are made.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Stakeholders label every requirement for the new product as "must have". What should the product owner do?
A) Facilitate prioritisation with clear rules, such as limiting must-haves to what is essential
B) Accept the labels and plan to build every requirement in the first release
C) Ask the developers to choose the requirements that are easiest to build first
D) Prioritise the requirements of the stakeholders who complain the loudest
= A
? Techniques such as MoSCoW only work when the categories have clear rules. Limiting "must have" to what is essential for a viable release makes the priorities meaningful.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
Stakeholders expect a cloud migration to reduce IT costs immediately. In reality, costs will rise for six months because the old and new systems will run in parallel. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the cost profile and agree when savings are expected to start
B) Shorten the parallel running period so that savings appear at once
C) Report only the long-term savings, since the temporary rise is unavoidable
D) Let the finance department explain the higher costs when they appear
= A
? Explaining the expected cost profile early, including the temporary rise, prevents surprise and keeps the business case credible.

@@ PE5 | Predictive | multi
Sales wants every planned feature in the new warehouse system before go-live, while operations wants a stable system first. Which two actions best align their expectations? (Choose two.)
A) Facilitate a joint session to agree measurable go-live criteria
B) Document the agreed criteria and trade-offs, approved by the sponsor
C) Accept the sales list, since revenue matters most to the business
D) Let each department test against its own set of criteria
E) Wait until the two departments reach agreement on their own
= A, B
? Expectations are aligned by agreeing shared, measurable criteria together and documenting them with the sponsor's approval.

@@ PE6 | Agile
After a redesign of the online shop, the conversion rate fell by 5%, and the sales director is furious. What should the product owner do?
A) Acknowledge the concern, analyse the data with stakeholders and agree corrective steps
B) Explain that conversion rates always fluctuate and that the redesign needs more time
C) Roll back every change made in the last six months to restore the old shop at once
D) Ask the sales director to raise the concern formally at the next quarterly review
= A
? Taking concerns seriously, analysing the evidence together and agreeing actions, such as experiments or a partial rollback, maintains trust and focuses on value.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
The renovation will finish two weeks late because of a supplier delay. The hotel owner has wedding bookings for those two weeks. What should the project manager do?
A) Inform the owner now, with the impact and options such as phased handover
B) Ask the contractor to finish on time, whatever the cost to quality or safety
C) Wait to see whether the supplier recovers part of the delay before saying anything
D) Tell the owner about the delay after the weddings have been moved elsewhere
= A
? Bad news is communicated promptly, with its impact and options, so that the stakeholder can make decisions in time.

@@ PE6 | Hybrid
The research sponsor keeps asking the team for additional analyses that are outside the agreed scope. What should the project manager do?
A) Discuss the requests, assess their impact and handle them through the agreed change process
B) Perform the analyses quietly, since the sponsor is the most important stakeholder
C) Refuse all of the requests, since the scope was agreed at the start of the project
D) Ask the team to work extra hours so that the additional analyses do not affect the plan
= A
? Requests outside scope are assessed and decided through the agreed process, so that their impact on time, cost and other work is visible and accepted.

@@ PE6 | Hybrid
After smart meters were installed, many customers complained that their bills went up, because the new meters measure usage more accurately. What should the project manager do?
A) Work with customer service to explain the change and offer bill reviews
B) Ask the engineers to recalibrate the meters so that the bills fall again
C) Tell customer service that billing complaints are outside the project's scope
D) Stop the rollout until the complaints have stopped completely
= A
? Managing expectations includes helping stakeholders understand changes that affect them, through clear explanations and support channels.

@@ PE6 | Agile
Satisfaction surveys show that stakeholders feel surprised by the changes they see at each sprint review. What should the product owner do?
A) Involve them earlier, for example in backlog refinement and previews of upcoming work
B) Show fewer changes at each review, so that the stakeholders are less surprised by them
C) Stop sending the surveys, since stakeholders will get used to the changes over time
D) Ask the sponsor to approve each change before it is shown at a sprint review
= A
? Early involvement makes upcoming changes expected and gives stakeholders the chance to shape them.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
During user acceptance testing, the customer rejects a deliverable because it lacks a function that the customer says was always expected. What should the project manager do first?
A) Review the requirements baseline and acceptance criteria with the customer
B) Add the function at once, since the customer must be satisfied at the end
C) Tell the customer that the function was never requested, so it will not be built
D) Escalate the dispute to the sponsor before discussing it with the customer
= A
? The agreed requirements and acceptance criteria are the objective basis for the discussion. If the function is new, it is handled as a change request.

@@ PE6 | Hybrid
An executive who supported the project has become resistant after hearing rumours that it will lead to job cuts in her department. What should the project manager do?
A) Meet her privately to understand her concerns, share the facts and update the engagement plan
B) Ask the sponsor to instruct the executive to continue supporting the project
C) Avoid the executive until the rumours have died down on their own
D) Tell the executive's team that the rumours are false, without checking the facts
= A
? A change in engagement is addressed directly. Understanding concerns, sharing accurate information and adjusting the engagement approach can restore support.

@@ PE7 | Hybrid
In a research lab, doctoral students leave every three to four years, and their experimental know-how is often lost. What should the project manager recommend?
A) Standardised protocols and lab notebooks, with new students paired with experienced ones
B) Asking each student to write a long report of everything they know on their last day
C) Keeping the same students on the project for longer by extending their contracts
D) Relying on the professors to remember the details of every experimental method
= A
? Combining explicit knowledge (protocols and notebooks) with tacit knowledge transfer (pairing) reduces the loss of know-how when people leave.

@@ PE7 | Predictive
A new property management system will be used by 1,200 staff in 30 hotels, and the training budget is limited. Which approach best transfers the knowledge?
A) Train-the-trainer with local super-users and job aids
B) A single classroom session at headquarters for all 1,200 staff
C) A user manual emailed to every hotel on the go-live date
D) Training only for the general managers of the 30 hotels
= A
? Training local super-users who then train and support their colleagues scales knowledge transfer and leaves expertise in each hotel.

@@ PE7 | Hybrid
The organisation wants to use a generative AI assistant to answer team members' questions from project documents. What must be ensured for this to work well?
A) The knowledge base is accurate and current, access rights are respected and answers are checked
B) The assistant is given access to all of the company's documents, whatever their confidentiality
C) Team members stop updating the documents, since the assistant will fill any gaps on its own
D) The answers are treated as final, since the assistant is faster than asking colleagues
= A
? AI tools are only as good as the knowledge they draw on. Data quality, access controls and human review of answers are essential.

@@ PE7 | Predictive
A senior field technician can tell from the sound of a pump that it is about to fail. How can this knowledge best be passed on to newer technicians?
A) Job shadowing and hands-on mentoring in the field
B) A written procedure describing the sounds of a failing pump
C) A recorded presentation about pump maintenance
D) A quiz on pump specifications for every technician
= A
? This is tacit knowledge, which is best transferred through observation, shared experience and mentoring rather than documents alone.

@@ PE7 | Predictive
The project identified several new risks during a complex supplier transition. How should this knowledge be made available to future projects?
A) By updating the organisation's risk checklists and templates
B) By keeping the project's risk register in the project manager's files
C) By mentioning the risks in the final project presentation only
D) By asking future project managers to contact this project's team
= A
? Updating organisational process assets, such as risk checklists and templates, turns project knowledge into reusable assets.

@@ PE7 | Agile
Which practice spreads knowledge within an agile team most effectively during day-to-day work?
A) Pairing, with partners rotated regularly
B) Assigning each module to one specialist
C) Writing documentation at the end of each release
D) Making code reviews optional to save time
= A
? Pairing with regular rotation spreads knowledge continuously and builds collective ownership. Single-specialist ownership creates knowledge silos.

@@ PE8 | Predictive | fill
A project team has 15 members, including the project manager. Five more people will join. How many additional communication channels will there be? (Answer with a number.)
= 85
? Channels = n(n − 1)/2. With 15 people: 15 × 14 / 2 = 105. With 20 people: 20 × 19 / 2 = 190. Additional channels = 190 − 105 = 85.

@@ PE8 | Hybrid
Hotel staff work in shifts and rarely read email. How should the project communicate updates about the new system to them?
A) Use shift briefings, notice boards and short messages on devices they already use
B) Send longer and more detailed emails so that the important updates are not missed
C) Ask the general manager to forward each email update to all of the hotel's staff
D) Communicate only with the department heads, who know everything their staff need
= A
? Communication methods should fit how the audience works. Shift briefings and channels that staff already use reach people where they are.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
A contractor damages a water main during meter installation, and 2,000 homes lose their water supply. Journalists call the project team for comments. Who should speak to the media?
A) The spokesperson named in the communications plan
B) The installer who was on site when the damage happened
C) Any team member who receives a call from a journalist
D) The contractor's site supervisor, since the contractor caused the damage
= A
? Crisis communication follows the agreed plan, with a designated spokesperson, so that messages are accurate, consistent and coordinated.

@@ PE8 | Agile
Which communication method is best for resolving a complex disagreement between two team members quickly?
A) Interactive communication, such as a conversation or video call
B) Push communication, such as a detailed email to both people
C) Pull communication, such as a page on the project wiki
D) A written memo from the project manager to both team members
= A
? Interactive communication allows immediate clarification and feedback, which is most effective for complex or sensitive topics.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
In the basic communication model, what is the term for anything that interferes with the transmission or understanding of a message, such as jargon or a poor connection?
A) Noise
B) Encoding
C) Feedback
D) Medium
= A
? Noise is anything that disrupts the message. Senders reduce it by choosing a suitable medium, avoiding jargon and confirming understanding.

@@ PE8 | Agile
Stakeholders want to see both the work completed and the changes in total scope over the course of a release. Which chart shows this best?
A) A release burnup chart
B) A sprint burndown chart
C) A Pareto chart
D) A scatter diagram
= A
? A burnup chart shows completed work and total scope as separate lines, so scope changes are visible. A burndown chart hides scope changes in a single line.

@@ PE8 | Hybrid | multi
Which two factors should influence the choice of communication method for a project's stakeholders? (Choose two.)
A) The urgency of the information
B) The stakeholders' location and access to technology
C) The project manager's personal preference
D) Whichever tool is the cheapest to use
E) The number of words in the message
= A, B
? Communication methods are chosen based on factors such as urgency, the audience's location and technology, the sensitivity of the information and the need for interaction.
